+## v0.15.9.11.1
+
+Windows low-level keyboard hook (`WH_KEYBOARD_LL`) for chase Auto key suppression. v0.15.9.11 BAT revealed that the engine-keyboard-buffer (kbBuf) zeroing layer in `HookedGetKeyState` was silently broken: Aaron pressed arrow keys during chase Auto, the arrows reached the engine, interrupted the auto-pilot, and a chase battle fired just 2 seconds after engagement.
+
+### Why kbBuf zeroing didn't work
+
+The v0.15.9.11 BAT mod log showed:
+
+```
+[13:14:15][MOD] FF8Addresses:   pKeyboardState (byte**) = 0x01CD02D8 (val=0x00000000)
+```
+
+The pointer resolved successfully, but its dereferenced value stayed `0x00000000` at all times. **FFNx replaces FF8's DirectInput keyboard path with its own implementation**, so the original engine keyboard buffer at `*0x01CD02D8` is never populated. The `if (kbBuf)` guard inside `HookedGetKeyState` always fell through and the zeroing block never ran -- neither the pre-existing v05.89 arrow-zeroing nor the v0.15.9.11 W/A/D/X extension. That's why neither activation log line ever appeared in the BAT log despite `s_analogOverrideActive=1` being confirmed at engagement.
+
+The v0.15.9.11 HandleKeys gate (Layer 2, for the mod's nav hotkeys -, =, Backspace, \) DID work -- those keys are read via `GetAsyncKeyState` from mod-owned code and don't depend on FFNx's keyboard path. So only Layer 1 (kbBuf) was affected.
+
+### Fix: Windows low-level keyboard hook
+
+A `WH_KEYBOARD_LL` hook sits ABOVE FFNx in the input chain. Physical key events come from the OS, hit our hook FIRST, and never reach FFNx or the engine if we swallow them. The hook is installed in `DllMain DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH` (on the game's main thread, which has the message pump LL hooks require) and uninstalled in `DLL_PROCESS_DETACH`.
+
+Key design points:
+
+- **Auto-pilot's SendInput-injected arrows pass through.** They're flagged `LLKHF_INJECTED` in `KBDLLHOOKSTRUCT.flags`; the hook only acts on physical events.
+- **Tight gate.** The hook only swallows when `ChaseAutoPilot::IsEngaged() && ChaseDetector::GetChaseMode() == MODE_AUTO`. Outside the chase it's a pure pass-through.
+- **O(1) hook proc.** Read flags, compare vkCode to ~8 constants, query two inline accessors. Well within the Windows LL hook timeout budget.
+- **Cross-thread safety without locks.** The hook fires on the game main thread; chase state is set on the accessibility worker thread. Both accessor returns (`bool` and `Mode` enum) are atomically readable on x86 -- no locks required.
+- **Scope matches Aaron's 2026-05-12 design clarification.** Swallow arrows + W/A/D/X. Preserve S (pause). F-keys (audio/TTS), V (version), G/T/L/R (stat lookups), mod nav hotkeys (-, =, Backspace, \), and all other keys flow through normally.
+
+### What's preserved
+
+The v0.15.9.11 dispatch-level gate in `field_nav_handlekeys.inl::HandleKeys` (which BAT confirmed working) is left in place. It handles the mod's GetAsyncKeyState-read nav hotkeys (-, =, Backspace, \) during chase Auto -- those don't go through the kbBuf or FFNx, so the LL hook doesn't reach them either; the HandleKeys early-return remains the right tool for that path.
+
+The v0.15.9.11 kbBuf zeroing block in `field_nav_input_hooks.inl::HookedGetKeyState` is left in place as no-cost belt-and-suspenders (it can't fire because `*pKeyboardState` is always null; harmless dead code). A future cleanup can remove it once we've collected enough chase BATs to be confident the LL hook fully covers the case.
+
+### Compatibility
+
+- **NVDA users:** NVDA doesn't intercept arrow keys for FF8 (per Aaron's project memory). Our LL hook fires on Aaron's physical arrow presses and swallows them during chase Auto. ✅
+- **JAWS users:** JAWS intercepts arrow keys until Insert+3 passthrough is engaged. That's a pre-existing JAWS behavior, not a regression introduced by this hook. ✅
+- **Mod F-keys and info hotkeys:** Reach the OS normally, `GetAsyncKeyState` sees them as pressed, mod handlers fire as before. ✅
+- **S key for pause:** Not in the suppressed set, FF8 sees it normally. ✅
+
+### Expected v0.15.9.11.1 BAT
+
+Aaron picks **Auto** at the chase ASK. Auto-pilot engages as in v0.15.9.10 Auto. During the chase, Aaron presses arrow keys -- nothing happens, party continues running per the auto-pilot's intended direction. F-keys still adjust audio/TTS rate/volume. S still pauses. No `[CBF] PASS` lines on chase fields. Chase completes through to the Lapin Beach FMV.
+
+Verification markers in logs:
+
+- `Logs/ff8_mod.log`: `WH_KEYBOARD_LL hook installed (handle=0x...)` near session start.
+- `Logs/ff8_field.log`: `[v0.15.9.11.1] LL keyboard hook first suppressed key vk=0x...` on Aaron's first physical arrow press during chase Auto.
+- v0.15.9.11's `HandleKeys suppressor active` line also fires as before (Layer 2 unchanged).
+- v0.15.9.10's `chase ACTIVATED ... mode=auto`, `ChaseAutoPilot: ENGAGED`, no `[CBF]` lines, `chase DEACTIVATED ... battles_fired=0` markers all apply.
+
+### Risk
+
+Low. The hook is fully optional -- if `SetWindowsHookExW` fails, we log a warning and continue with the v0.15.9.11 layers (which still cover Layer 2). The swallow branch is tightly gated on chase Auto being engaged, so outside the chase it's a pure pass-through. Worst case (an unexpected key Aaron wants is in the suppressed set): remove it from `LowLevelKeyboardProc`'s `isArrow || isGameKey` check and re-BAT.
+
