field_dialog.cpp's ScanAndSpeakChoiceWindows hook (which runs for every opcode_ask) iterates over ALL 8 dialog window slots looking for ASK choice fields. When the chase ASK opens in slot 2, the hook also visits slot 0 (which still holds Squall's chase-trigger MES from 3 seconds earlier). Slot 0's firstQ=0xFF and lastQ=0xFF -- FF8's "no ASK fields set" sentinel -- but the existing skip predicates (firstQ==0 && lastQ==0; lastQ < firstQ) didn't catch the (0xFF, 0xFF) case. The slot got decoded as a 0-choice dialog with a non-empty prompt, then the prompt was spoken as if it were the ASK. Result: "Let's go!" gets announced once by AMESW when the MES fires, then again by the [ASK] hook when the chase ASK opens. v0.15.9.9.1 adds `if (firstQ == 0xFF || lastQ == 0xFF) continue;` to ScanAndSpeakChoiceWindows so windows with the FF8 unset sentinel are correctly skipped. 0xFF is unambiguously a sentinel (FF8 dialogs cap at ~16 choices, so neither firstQ nor lastQ can be 0xFF in a real ASK). Risk: very low. One-line additive predicate. Worst case (we missed a legitimate ASK with one of the fields set to 0xFF): the slot's text won't be read out, which is a quieter regression than the duplicate. Files: src/field_dialog.cpp (sentinel check in ScanAndSpeakChoiceWindows around line 681), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.9: VERIFICATION BUILD -- combines ASK text polish (item #4) and battle-suppressor disable in Auto mode (item #1). Two coordinated changes: (1) chase_battle_freeze.cpp: AUTO-mode cap raised from 0 to INT_MAX so any chase battle the auto-pilot fails to avoid will PASS through to a real battle screen instead of being silently NO-OP'd. The v0.15.9.8.3 BAT recorded 0 [CBF] NO-OP lines on the whole chase, but that didn't prove whether the underlying chase battle calls were 0 (auto-pilot doing the work) or N>0 (suppressor band-aid doing the work). v0.15.9.9 BAT answers the question: if 0 [CBF] PASS lines appear, the auto-pilot is fully sufficient and the suppressor can be removed in v0.15.10; if any PASS fires, it's a real regression to investigate. (2) chase_ask_overlay.cpp: prompt changed from "Mode?" to "X-ATM092 is heading right for you. How do you want to run?" (replaces the redundant Squall "Let's go!" line at ASK open with situational context). Option labels updated to describe what each does now that Auto is fully implemented: Manual="drive yourself, one battle per field", Auto="mod drives, no battles or shake", Original="vanilla chase, no mod help" (Original still falls back to Manual until v0.15.9.10 implements MODE_ORIGINAL; label change is deferred to that version so we don't promise what the build doesn't deliver). Predicted v0.15.9.9 BAT: ChaseBattleFreeze Initialize log shows "cap=INT_MAX in AUTO (VERIFICATION BUILD)", ASK announces new explainer + new option labels on cursor change, full chase completes with 0 [CBF] PASS and 0 [CBF] NO-OP lines on any chase field. Risk: low. Single-constant change in chase_battle_freeze + 4 string changes in chase_ask_overlay. Worst case (a real chase battle fires): revert cap=INT_MAX -> 0 in v0.15.9.9.1 to restore the band-aid while we diagnose. Files: src/chase_battle_freeze.cpp (cap constant, log message, <climits> include), src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (prompt string, kChaseChoices labels), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.8.3: Bridge dance + kani-slot override (domt1_1). Ships TWO coordinated changes targeting the bridge's remaining 1 catch in v0.15.9.8.2 BAT. (1) chase_detector.cpp ApplyPerFieldKaniOverride() runs after the default SYM-name-based kani slot resolution and, on field == 'domt1_1', overrides s_kaniLoc to Others slot 3 (SYM 'laguna'). v0.15.9.8.2 BAT BridgeDiag (416 samples over 6.7 seconds) empirically proved the actually-pursuing entity is at this slot -- ChaseDetector's default 'kani' SYM resolves to Others slot 6 which reads (0,0) the entire transit. The bridge field reuses a generic NPC SYM template for X-ATM092. With the override in place, ReadKaniPosition() and the per-second tick log both read the correct entity for the first time on this field. (2) chase_auto_pilot.cpp adds MODE_BRIDGE_DANCE, a new drive mode for domt1_1 only. Per-tick (10Hz) state machine samples kani.X and classifies motion: LEAPING (deltaX > 200/tick), LANDED (deltaX < 50/tick), CHASING (everything between). Two states: EAST_LEG (initial, drive east) -> turn west when kani lands IN FRONT of party with the wasLeaping latch true; WEST_LEG (drive west) -> turn east the instant a new leap STARTS (mid-air), so party slips past while robot can't course-correct. Aaron's mechanic, verbatim: "Robot jumps east, lands in front of party. Wait for robot to LAND before turning west. ... After turning west, robot eventually jumps again. As soon as the robot jumps (mid-air), turn east immediately -- no wait for landing." Detection signals from v0.15.9.8.2 BAT: kani Y is constant at -446 throughout (jumps are X-axis only in walkmesh coords; visual jump is 3D Z), normal pursuit ~106 units/100ms tick, leap speed ~372 units/100ms tick (3.5x), landed 0-1 units/tick. Thresholds: kBridgeLeapThreshold=200, kBridgeLandThreshold=50, kBridgeLandConsec=2 (samples), kBridgeMinDwellTicks=60 (1s), kBridgeWestTimeoutTicks=300 (5s). The west-leg timeout is the safety net: empirically we don't have data on what the robot does AFTER the party turns west (the v0.15.9.8.2 catch fired before any turn), so we hedge with a 5s bail-back-to-east if no leap fires. Worst-case behavior with timeout firing: identical to v0.15.9.8.2 (1 catch at X~2053 then post-catch teleport advances party). BridgeDiag from v0.15.9.8.2 remains active for one more BAT cycle so we can confirm the slot-3 kani is consistent and capture the previously-missing west-leg robot trajectory. Predicted v0.15.9.8.3 BAT outcome: ENGAGED mode=BRIDGE_DANCE log line on domt1_1, ChaseDetector OVERRIDE log line on field-debounce settle, BridgeDance per-sample logs at 10Hz throughout transit, EAST->WEST transition log after ~6 seconds when laguna lands, then either (best case) WEST->EAST transition on second leap with 0 catches; or (acceptable) WEST->EAST TIMEOUT after 5s and 0-1 catches; or (worst) some unforeseen interaction. Risk: medium. The west-leg detection is inferred from Aaron's verbal description and has no empirical backing yet -- the BAT will give us that data. The east-leg detection is fully calibrated from v0.15.9.8.2 BAT signals. Kani-slot override is surgically scoped to domt1_1; other chase fields keep default ChaseDetector resolution. Files: src/chase_detector.cpp (ApplyPerFieldKaniOverride + call site), src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (MODE_BRIDGE_DANCE enum value, kBridgeXxx constants, BridgeDanceState type, s_bridgeXxx state vars, UpdateBridgeDance helper, Engage/Disengage/Initialize handling, explicit domt1_1 kFieldConfigs entry, ENGAGED log case, IsDirectionLikeMode update, Initialize log message), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
   // v0.15.9.8.2: DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY build for the bridge (domt1_1). v0.15.9.8.1 BAT brought total chase catches from 11 (baseline) to 1, with only the mid-east-traversal catch on domt1_1 remaining. Aaron described the bridge mechanic (2026-05-12): the robot jumps east in front of the party, party must wait for it to LAND then turn west, robot eventually jumps again in front (or back over), party must IMMEDIATELY turn east when the jump starts (without waiting for landing); turning prematurely during the east leg causes the robot to run the party down and trigger battle. The asymmetric rule maps to a single Y-axis signal: bridge is a horizontal corridor at Y ~= -607 (confirmed from v0.15.9.8.1 BAT party trajectory), so `abs(kani.Y - party.Y) < tolerance` means "landed on corridor" (east-leg turn condition) and `abs(kani.Y - party.Y) > tolerance` means "airborne / off corridor" (west-leg turn condition). Problem: ChaseDetector currently resolves kani symIdx=12 to Others slot 6 on domt1_1, but that slot reads pos=(0,0) on every per-tick log throughout the v0.15.9.8.1 BAT bridge traversal. Either (a) the bridge uses a different entity for the actively-jumping kani than ChaseDetector's symbol resolution finds, or (b) the resolution is correct but the entity's position is stored at a different offset on this field. v0.15.9.8.2 adds a per-tick (10Hz) diagnostic on domt1_1 that enumerates every non-zero slot of both pFieldStateOthers and pFieldStateBackgrounds, dumping slot index + SYM name + world pos + Y-delta-vs-party for each. BAT analysis identifies the real moving entity; v0.15.9.8.3 then routes ChaseDetector to it and implements the asymmetric dance. **Y-AXIS THEORY REFUTED 2026-05-12**: v0.15.9.8.2 BAT BridgeDiag empirically proved Y is constant on the bridge -- the moving kani at Others slot 3 (SYM 'laguna') has Y=-446 throughout, party has Y=-607 throughout, so ydelta is a useless constant of 161. The jump signal is X-velocity (3.5x normal speed during leap, 0 during landing). v0.15.9.8.3 thresholds were derived from this. No behavior change to the bridge in this build -- chase still runs as in v0.15.9.8.1 with 1 catch. Risk: very low. Pure additive logging, hard-gated to fieldName == "domt1_1". Files: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (LogBridgeDiagnostic helper + per-tick call + counter), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md.
   // v0.15.9.8.1 [BAT SUCCESS 2026-05-12]: Bump doopen2a targetY from -3703 to -3800 (97 units past the south trigger line at y ~= -3700). v0.15.9.8 BAT showed the south-target fix worked structurally on doopen2a (party ran 3300+ units south in 6s with no NW detour) but A* stopped 159 units short of the trigger line at pos=(-958,-3541) -- likely the last reachable walkmesh waypoint from the requested target (-952,-3703). Party sat there for 8 seconds with chase-drive still active, the chase script's catch evaluator fired once at 23:04:28 forcing 1 [CBF] catch on this field, and the field eventually transitioned at 23:04:34 via the chase script's natural progression after the catch. v0.15.9.8 result: town square went 2 -> 1 catch (-1), bridge unchanged at 1, total chase 3 -> 2 catches. v0.15.9.8.1 hypothesis: by targeting -3800 instead of -3703, A*'s last reachable waypoint should fall closer to or past the trigger line itself, letting the party actually cross the line and fire the script-side field transition before the catch evaluator window expires. If walkmesh extends past the trigger, A* will find a path further south. If walkmesh literally ends at -3541, A* will clip to that same point as v0.15.9.8 and we'll see no improvement -- in which case v0.15.9.8.2 switches to MODE_DIRECTION south (skipping path-finding entirely, like dotown_2 and dotown_1). Single-coordinate edit; risk negligible. Predicted v0.15.9.8.1 BAT outcome (SUCCESS): doopen2a 0 catches, transit 6-8s, clean transition to dotown_3 via south trigger crossing. Files: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (targetY -3703 -> -3800 + rationale comment), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md.
   // v0.15.9.8: Explicit MODE_TARGET config for doopen2a (Dollet Town Square). v0.15.9.7.8 BAT [CBF] count by field showed 2 catches on doopen2a, root-caused via the field log: doopen2a has ZERO INF gateways, so the BuildFallbackConfig fallback chain dropped to tier 2 (TRIGGER-LINE). The field has two SETLINE trigger lines: idx=1 center (-1390,854) NW and idx=257 center (-952,-3703) SOUTH. GetTriggerLineNearestCluster picked the NW one because the walkmesh's largest dead-end cluster lands at (-1315,503) -- an interior corner. So the auto-pilot targeted NW while the field actually needs the party to run south. v0.15.9.8 added an explicit MODE_TARGET entry for doopen2a targeting the south trigger line center (-952,-3703), bypassing the cluster heuristic entirely. v0.15.9.8 BAT result: 2 -> 1 catches on doopen2a (50% reduction). Remaining issue diagnosed in v0.15.9.8.1.
